<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW is the whole app. It is a **special** permission, not a runtime one:
        there is no dialog Gloam can raise, only a settings screen it can send the user to, and the
        answer has to be re-read on every resume because it can be revoked there just as easily.
        `shade/OverlayPermission.kt` is the only place that asks.

        POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a runtime permission on API 33+ and granted at install below it. The
        shade's foreground service is required by Android to show a notification, and on 33+ a
        denied permission means it shows none — the service still runs, but the user loses the Stop
        action that is their way out of a very dark screen.

        FOREGROUND_SERVICE and its `specialUse` type: the shade has to outlive Gloam's own window,
        because the point is to dim *other* apps. None of the platform's named types (camera,
        location, mediaPlayback, …) describes drawing an overlay, which is what `specialUse` exists
        for — and Play requires the justification below to be filled in for it.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />

    <!--
        RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED is owned by reboot restore — `shade/BootReceiver.kt`. It is a
        **normal** permission: no dialog, no runtime ask, granted at install, and the only thing it
        buys is being told the phone has started.

        **Declared rather than inherited, and this line is now the only thing declaring it.** Until
        Phase 2 removed WorkManager the permission was in the built artifact anyway, merged in by a
        library rather than written by us — one dependency bump away from disappearing, and it would
        have disappeared *silently*: the receiver would simply stop being called, on a feature nobody
        tests by rebooting their phone. Declaring it first and removing the merge afterwards is the
        order that never left a gap. `aab-permissions.py` reads the artifact rather than this file
        precisely because merged permissions are invisible from here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
        Package visibility (API 30+) for the one OEM screen the app offers to open: Xiaomi's
        autostart manager, which on HyperOS decides whether background work runs at all. Without
        this declaration `resolveActivity` returns null on a phone where the activity exists, and
        the app would either hide a button that works or show one that does nothing.

        A named package, not QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES: we look for exactly one thing and say which.

        The three <intent> entries are for hand-offs to a mail app, Play and a browser. None of the
        launches needs them — package visibility has never blocked `startActivity`, and each launch
        is a try/catch around `ActivityNotFoundException`. What visibility *does* block is
        `resolveActivity`, which would answer "no mail app" on every phone that has one. Nothing
        performs such a pre-check today; these entries exist so one added later cannot silently lie.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.miui.securitycenter" />

        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.SENDTO" />
            <data android:scheme="mailto" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="market" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <!--
        `android:localeConfig` is read by Android 13+ only, which is every device this app ships to
        now that `minSdk` is 33 (ADR-0008) — so it no longer needs a tools:targetApi to silence
        lint's "unused below minSdk" note. It is the one declaration of the shipped languages that
        the framework reads (ADR-0004); `AppLanguage` and `TranslationTest` are the other two, and
        the test is what holds all three to the same claim.

        **AppCompat's below-13 locale backport was here and is gone** — a disabled
        `AppLocalesMetadataHolderService` entry carrying `autoStoreLocales`, which existed so
        AppCompat could persist a language choice on devices with no per-app locale store of their
        own. `minSdk` 33 means the app ships to none of them, so on every device it reaches the
        framework persists the choice and the entry was ignored. Removed as a dated amendment on
        ADR-0004, not as a tidy-up. **AppCompat itself stays**: ADR-0006 needs `setDefaultNightMode`,
        and ADR-0004's switcher is `AppCompatDelegate.setApplicationLocales`.

        Auto Backup is the platform's default here, with no agent and no rules XML.

        The template shipped an `AppBackupAgent` because static include/exclude XML cannot checkpoint
        a database's write-ahead log, and without that the platform is eligible to copy the live
        database and its `-wal` sidecar mid-write — a backup that appears to work and restores
        corrupt. Gloam has no database, so it has no WAL, so it has nothing to checkpoint and no
        reason to take over its own file set. What is left to back up is the DataStore preferences
        file, which the platform's default handles correctly on its own (ADR-0005).
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".MainApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.App">
        <!--
            **The launcher entry is not here. It is on `ControlsActivity`** — shape iv in
            `docs/phase-3.md` §2, and the reason is a platform fact rather than taste.

            While this Activity held the entry, the icon opened the compact controls by forwarding:
            read the preference in `onCreate`, start the dialog, finish. That works from cold and
            **cannot** work once the app's task exists in the background, because a launcher tap on
            an existing task is a `moveTaskToFront` — the platform brings the *already composed*
            window forward and puts it on screen before a line of our code runs, and only then
            delivers `onNewIntent` for the forward to fire. What flashed was the full app itself,
            not a placeholder, which is why `android:windowDisablePreview` here removed the cold
            start's starting window and left the flash exactly where it was. Read on the phone:
            `am start` with `CATEGORY_LAUNCHER` answers *"Activity not started, its current task has
            been brought to the front"*.

            No forward can outrun that, so the fix is that the icon's intent never resolves into
            this task: it names `ControlsActivity`, whose empty `taskAffinity` puts it somewhere
            else entirely. `windowDisablePreview` goes with the forward — with the launcher landing
            elsewhere, a cold start of the *full* app is a start the user asked for, and it gets its
            placeholder back rather than showing the launcher until Compose has a frame.

            `singleTop` stays. Nothing forwards into this Activity from the launcher any more, but
            the cog in the compact controls and the one in the panel both start it with
            `FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK`, and this is what keeps a second tap from stacking a second
            copy on the task — which is correct anyway for an app whose whole UI is one Activity and
            a Nav3 back stack.

            **`exported="true"` with no intent filter is deliberate.** There is no Android Studio in
            this loop: `scripts/project.py` publishes this component as `MAIN_ACTIVITY`,
            `scripts/edge-to-edge.py` drives the whole screenshot matrix through `am start -n`, and
            R2 read on this phone that `am start -n` cannot reach a component that is not exported.
            What it opens is the app's own first screen — no side effect an outside caller could
            trigger by opening it.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:exported="true" />

        <!--
            The compact controls (`docs/phase-3.md` §2). A second activity rather than a flag on
            MainActivity, because the thing that makes a window float is the **manifest** theme,
            which the platform reads before any of this app's code runs — one activity has one of
            those, and the starting window is drawn from it either way. That asymmetry is also why
            the launcher entry moved here rather than staying on `MainActivity` with a forward.

            **This is where the launcher icon lands**, and the four task attributes below are what
            make that safe rather than what make it tidy. `taskAffinity=""` is the load-bearing one
            now: it is why a tap on the icon cannot resolve into `MainActivity`'s task and bring the
            full app forward, which is the flash shape iii could not get rid of. `noHistory` means
            somebody who opens the controls and leaves does not find them again on top of whatever
            they switch to next; `excludeFromRecents` keeps a dialog out of the recents list; and
            `singleTop` makes a second tap reuse this window rather than stack a second one behind
            the first — and delivers `onNewIntent`, which is where the preference gets a second
            reading (`ControlsActivity.onNewIntent`).

            **Nothing of this window is drawn before its own content is**, which is the other half
            of why the icon lands here: `Theme.App.Controls` is floating and translucent, so the
            platform gives it `STARTING_WINDOW_TYPE_NONE` and paints no placeholder at all. There is
            nothing to flash — the launcher stays on screen until the bar itself fades in.

            `exported="true"` is not a choice made here; it is what a launcher entry *is*. The cost
            is that any app can now open this dialog, and it is small: what appears is Gloam's own
            controls, over the caller's own screen, doing nothing at all until the person holding
            the phone taps something. The doors that mattered before — the shade's notification and
            2b's tile — are still this app's own `PendingIntent`s and never needed the export.

            **A launcher tap is told apart from every other route by its category, not by an extra.**
            `CATEGORY_LAUNCHER` is on the icon's intent and on nothing this app sends itself, so the
            *Open the small controls from the icon* preference is read on exactly the entry it is
            about, and the notification keeps opening the controls whatever the preference says.

            Because the task affinity is empty, the way *out* of here has to say so:
            `openFullApp()` adds FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK, or the full app would open inside this
            dialog's task and inherit its exclusion from recents.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ControlsActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.App.Controls"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:noHistory="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
            The shade. `exported="false"` because nothing outside this app has any business starting
            it — the notification's Stop action is a PendingIntent, which carries this app's own
            identity and needs no export.

            `foregroundServiceType="specialUse"` is required from Android 14, and the `<property>`
            under it is the justification string Play reads. None of the platform's named types
            covers "draws a dimming overlay", which is precisely the case specialUse exists for.

            **`stopWithTask="false"`** is the load-bearing attribute: without it, swiping Gloam out
            of the recents list takes the shade down with it. That is the opposite of what someone
            reading in the dark wants — they dim the screen and then leave the app, which is the
            normal way to use this.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".shade.ShadeService"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse"
            android:stopWithTask="false">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Draws a user-controlled dimming overlay above other apps, which must
                    remain on screen while the user is in those apps." />
        </service>

        <!--
            Reboot restore. `BOOT_COMPLETED` and `MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED` are two of the three
            documented exemptions from the ban on starting a foreground service from the background,
            and Android 15's blocklist for the first does not reach `specialUse` — the reasoning, and
            what the receiver refuses to do, is in `shade/BootReceiver.kt`.

            **`exported="true"` is required rather than chosen**: both of these are system broadcasts,
            and a receiver the system cannot reach is a receiver that never runs. It also makes the
            feature testable without a reboot — `am broadcast -a android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED`
            reaches it, which is R3. What another app could do with that is put back a shade the user
            themselves asked for, on a phone where they have already granted the overlay permission.

            **`directBootAware` is left at its default of false, deliberately.** Gloam's DataStore
            lives in credential-encrypted storage, so before the first unlock there is nothing for
            this receiver to read — which is also why `LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED` is not in the filter.

            No `MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED` data element: that broadcast is delivered only to the app that
            was replaced, so it needs no package filter to be about us.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".shade.BootReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
            The nightly schedule's alarm lands here. **`exported="false"` and no `<intent-filter>`,
            which is the opposite of the receiver above and for the opposite reason**: nothing but
            this app's own `PendingIntent` — held by `AlarmManager` on our behalf — ever sends to it,
            and a receiver that can raise a window over every other app should be reachable by
            nothing else.

            It starts a foreground service from the background like `BootReceiver` does, but it is
            not one of the three broadcasts that are documented exemptions from the ban. What
            licenses it is the user's battery-optimisation exemption putting Gloam on the power
            allowlist — measured rather than assumed, in `docs/phase-4.md` section 1 — which is why
            section 7 asks for that grant and `shade/ScheduleReceiver.kt` names the exception it
            throws without it.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".shade.ScheduleReceiver"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:exported="false" />
    </application>

</manifest>
